# Expected classification of tests/corpus/synthetic_small.log (one row per stream line).
# Columns: seq <TAB> primary <TAB> labels (comma-separated, alphabetical; '-' = none).
#
# STATE: T5.1 - validators, context keywords and the confidence model of spec 5.1-5.3
# with tau = 0.5, plus the correlator of spec 6.
# Derived with tools/ref_classify.py, an independent Python implementation of spec 3.4
# and 5.1-5.3 (run_e2e.sh re-derives it and fails if this file is stale). Differences
# from the v0 file (no confidence model) were reviewed line by line:
#   - seq 111 (bad Luhn), 117 and 119 (bad Verhoeff), 168 (14-digit timestamp) lose PII:
#     their validators fail on every span;
#   - seq 107 (valid card) keeps PII but its overlapping Aadhaar match is discarded;
#   - seq 31 and 33 (bare HTTP 403/401) lose AUTH_FAILURE: HTTP_401_403 has base 0.35,
#     below tau on its own (it needs a second AUTH signal);
#   - seq 89-92 lose PERF_WARNING: TIMEOUT has base 0.35 and no context keywords, so a
#     timeout line alone is below tau (this includes the annotation lines 89 and 91,
#     which name the rule, and the configuration line 92 the corpus marks as weak).
# T5.1 appended the correlator section (seq 179-217); rows 0-178 are unchanged:
#   - seq 192, the fifth failure from 192.0.2.66 in 28 s, gains SECURITY_THREAT (BRUTE_FORCE);
#   - seq 194, the login from that address right after, is SECURITY_THREAT (FAIL_THEN_SUCCESS);
#   - seq 206, the fifth distinct user from 192.0.2.77 in 244 s, gains SECURITY_THREAT (SPRAY);
#   - seq 209-217 (four failures, then a login) stay below the threshold.
